From: =?UTF-8?q?John=20T=C3=B6rnblom?= <john.tornblom@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:41:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/28] ac: write PA_CL_NANINF_CNTL on GFX6-9 whatever
 CLEAR_STATE gives

The GFX6-9 preamble leaves PA_CL_NANINF_CNTL to CLEAR_STATE. On the PS4,
whose CLEAR_STATE is the system's, the orbis-ports mesa-ps4 port read
the context registers back after RADV's preamble had run: eight of the
nine the preamble leaves to CLEAR_STATE held what it would have written,
but PA_CL_NANINF_CNTL held 0x8b47008d -- twelve bits set outside every
field of the register, so a value nobody chose -- with VTE_XY_INF_DISCARD
and VTE_W_INF_DISCARD among them, which make the rasterizer discard
primitives with an infinite vertex.

The preamble writes it, 0 as everywhere else: the GFX10 and GFX12
preambles always write it, and so does GFX6-9's without CLEAR_STATE.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Bb93U7uY5qVNuEcHDggEfE
---
 src/amd/common/ac_cmdbuf.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/amd/common/ac_cmdbuf.c b/src/amd/common/ac_cmdbuf.c
index 3b34709..de856cc 100644
--- a/src/amd/common/ac_cmdbuf.c
+++ b/src/amd/common/ac_cmdbuf.c
@@ -255,8 +255,13 @@ gfx6_init_graphics_preamble_state(const struct ac_preamble_state *state,
    if (!info->has_clear_state)
       ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028A1C_VGT_HOS_MIN_TESS_LEVEL, fui(0));
 
+   /* Written whatever CLEAR_STATE gives: the PS4's leaves 0x8b47008d there (measured in the orbis-ports
+    * mesa-ps4 port, with bits set outside every field of the register), whose VTE_XY_INF_DISCARD and
+    * VTE_W_INF_DISCARD discard primitives with an infinite vertex.
+    */
+   ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028820_PA_CL_NANINF_CNTL, 0);
+
    if (!info->has_clear_state) {
-      ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028820_PA_CL_NANINF_CNTL, 0);
       ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028AC0_DB_SRESULTS_COMPARE_STATE0, 0x0);
       ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028AC4_DB_SRESULTS_COMPARE_STATE1, 0x0);
       ac_pm4_set_reg(pm4, R_028AC8_DB_PRELOAD_CONTROL, 0x0);
